<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pekan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pekan is the royal town of the Malaysian state of Pahang, near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahang-river">Pahang River</a> on the east coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a>. It was the seat of the first sultans of Pahang from around 1470 and later of the Bendahara rulers and the modern sultans, whose palace stands there. It is also the burial place of the Pahang royal family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pekan.jpg" alt="Pekan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Slleong · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pekan_13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Local lore holds that the town is named after the Bunga Pekan, a white flower like jasmine that once grew wild along the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahang-river">Pahang River</a> and is said to be extinct; other sources derive the name from Pekan Sehari, a Sunday market that still exists. Pekan is also the name of the district around the town and of a parliamentary constituency. The town lies about fifteen minutes from the sandy beaches of the coast, and Lake Chini, one of the largest freshwater lakes in Malaysia, is an hour's drive away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Pekan is thought to have existed since the Old Pahang Kingdom, when the town was called Inderapura, or Pura for short. The old capital lay on both banks of the Pahang and the Pahang Tua rivers as far as Tanjung Langgar, and Arab and Chinese writers mentioned it.</p><p>The town was divided into Pekan Lama, Old Pekan, where the first shops were built and some original wooden shophouses still stand, and Pekan Baharu, once known as Kampung Cina. In the late 19th century the sultan and the villagers were close: when a gong was struck in the palace, villagers gathered for his service, for example to join him hunting. The people of Kampong Mengkasar nearby are said to descend from Tok Tuan of Makassar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>.</p><p>Under British rule a residence for the British Resident was built in 1929, and the Chiefs' Rest House, a wooden guest house for the chiefs of Pahang attending the palace, was completed in 1926. The sultan acquired the Resident's house in 1948 as Kota Beram Palace, moving in 1965 to the new Abu Bakar Palace. From the early 1970s, under Tun Abdul Razak Hussein, the second Prime Minister of Malaysia, who was born in Pekan, the town became a base for vehicle assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pekan is the royal capital of Pahang and the home of its royal family. Its landmarks include the Royal Palace, the Royal Mausoleum, the Masjid Abdullah of the 1920s, the Abu Bakar Royal Mosque and the Sultan Abu Bakar Museum, opened in October 1976 in the former Resident's house. A campus of Universiti Malaysia Pahang Al-Sultan Abdullah stands there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pekan, Pahang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1470 Sultan Mansur Shah of Malacca installed his son Muhammad Shah as its first sultan, with his court at <b>Pekan</b> near the mouth of the Pahang River.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-pahang-sultanate">Old Pahang Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1770 it was granted as a hereditary fief to the Johor Bendahara, Tun Abdul Majid, whose descendants ruled from <b>Pekan</b> as Raja Bendahara.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahang-sultanate">Pahang Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
